1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,092
Lesson 8.1.

2
00:00:02,472 --> 00:00:05,217
Information geometry and
the natural gradient.

3
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:09,205
Gradient descent takes
steps in the steepest

4
00:00:09,205 --> 00:00:12,773
direction, but it measures
distance in parameter space.

5
00:00:13,153 --> 00:00:16,042
And parameters are an
arbitrary choice: the same

6
00:00:16,042 --> 00:00:18,450
model can be written in a thousand ways.

7
00:00:18,830 --> 00:00:20,739
What really matters is how much the

8
00:00:20,739 --> 00:00:23,249
distribution represented
by the model changes.

9
00:00:23,629 --> 00:00:27,806
Measuring steps that way leads to
the natural gradient, and solving it

10
00:00:27,806 --> 00:00:32,103
requires exactly what thermodynamic
hardware does well: a linear system.

11
00:00:33,217 --> 00:00:38,924
If we move the parameters from θ
to θ + d, the KL divergence between

12
00:00:38,924 --> 00:00:43,876
the old and new distribution
is, for small steps, ½ dᵀ F d.

13
00:00:44,256 --> 00:00:47,979
The matrix F is the Fisher
information: the expected

14
00:00:47,979 --> 00:00:51,702
outer product of the gradient
of the log-likelihood.

15
00:00:52,082 --> 00:00:55,740
It is a metric: it says how far
apart two nearby models are,

16
00:00:55,740 --> 00:00:58,910
measured by their predictions,
not by their numbers.

17
00:01:00,017 --> 00:01:03,666
The natural gradient is the
direction of steepest descent of

18
00:01:03,666 --> 00:01:06,830
the loss when the step size
is measured with the KL.

19
00:01:07,210 --> 00:01:13,614
Solving that problem with Lagrange
multipliers gives g̃ = F⁻¹ ∇ℓ.

20
00:01:13,994 --> 00:01:18,798
The update rule is θ ← θ − η F⁻¹ ∇ℓ.

21
00:01:19,178 --> 00:01:23,148
An important consequence: in the
small-step limit, the natural

22
00:01:23,148 --> 00:01:26,798
gradient does not depend on
how we parametrize the model.

23
00:01:27,917 --> 00:01:32,519
Let's fit a Gaussian with mean μ and
standard deviation σ to data with

24
00:01:32,519 --> 00:01:36,595
mean two and standard deviation
0.1, starting at zero and one.

25
00:01:36,975 --> 00:01:41,036
The Fisher information is diagonal:
one over sigma squared for the

26
00:01:41,036 --> 00:01:44,728
mean, and two over sigma squared
for the standard deviation.

27
00:01:45,108 --> 00:01:48,484
When σ shrinks, the
loss becomes very steep

28
00:01:48,484 --> 00:01:51,468
and gradient descent needs tiny steps.

29
00:01:51,848 --> 00:01:57,255
The natural gradient multiplies by
σ² and corrects exactly that effect.

30
00:01:58,383 --> 00:02:03,201
The result: with the best fixed learning
rate, gradient descent needs one

31
00:02:03,201 --> 00:02:07,293
hundred six steps to get within
one thousandth of the optimum.

32
00:02:07,673 --> 00:02:11,421
The natural gradient, with rate
one, does it in seven steps.

33
00:02:11,801 --> 00:02:16,653
The trajectories are also different:
ordinary gradient makes a long arc

34
00:02:16,653 --> 00:02:21,709
and then creeps along the narrow valley;
the natural one fixes the mean in

35
00:02:21,709 --> 00:02:25,809
a single step and then adjusts
the deviation in a few steps.

36
00:02:26,917 --> 00:02:28,215
Why isn't it always used?

37
00:02:28,595 --> 00:02:31,349
Because F is an N × N matrix, with N the

38
00:02:31,349 --> 00:02:34,380
number of parameters,
which can be millions.

39
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:39,079
Forming it costs N² and
solving the system, N³.

40
00:02:39,459 --> 00:02:43,275
That is why popular optimizers
use cheap approximations, such as

41
00:02:43,275 --> 00:02:46,793
Adam, which uses only the
diagonal of the empirical Fisher.

42
00:02:47,173 --> 00:02:50,335
The next lessons show how a thermodynamic

43
00:02:50,335 --> 00:02:53,575
device solves the full system efficiently.

44
00:02:54,683 --> 00:02:59,312
The natural gradient appears in neural
network training, in the natural

45
00:02:59,312 --> 00:03:04,005
policy gradient of reinforcement
learning, and in variational inference.

46
00:03:04,385 --> 00:03:08,190
In this unit we will see how to
make it affordable: conjugate

47
00:03:08,190 --> 00:03:11,683
gradient, Woodbury, the
thermodynamic version and K-FAC.

48
00:03:12,783 --> 00:03:16,100
Common mistakes: confusing
the Fisher with the Hessian.

49
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:19,201
Forgetting damping, when
the Fisher is singular

50
00:03:19,201 --> 00:03:21,805
because there are fewer
data than parameters.

51
00:03:22,185 --> 00:03:24,985
Expecting exact invariance
with finite steps.

52
00:03:25,365 --> 00:03:30,183
And believing Adam is a natural-gradient
method, when it uses only a diagonal.

53
00:03:31,283 --> 00:03:36,935
In summary: the Fisher information is
the local metric of the KL, and the

54
00:03:36,935 --> 00:03:42,045
natural gradient F⁻¹ ∇ℓ is steepest
descent in distribution space.

55
00:03:42,425 --> 00:03:45,899
In the Gaussian fit, seven
steps against one hundred six.

56
00:03:46,279 --> 00:03:49,357
Its cost is a linear
system of size N, the task

57
00:03:49,357 --> 00:03:52,106
thermodynamic hardware knows how to solve.

58
00:03:53,217 --> 00:03:57,390
To practice: derive the quadratic
approximation of the KL.

59
00:03:57,770 --> 00:03:59,583
Compute the Fisher of the Gaussian.

60
00:03:59,963 --> 00:04:03,411
Derive the natural gradient
with a Lagrange multiplier.

61
00:04:03,791 --> 00:04:05,998
And say which part of
the Fisher Adam uses.
